        "point": "Contextual intent: 'West Side Story' Quintet vs. First-Species Textbook",
        "detail": "The prompt explicitly grounds the question in Bernstein-Sondheim's *West Side Story*, a real-world dramatic polyphonic work, not an abstract, rhythmic species counterpoint exercise. The question asks 'Contrary motion occurs Z times'—in analysis of musical literature, 'contrary motion' refers to the deployment of the contrapuntal technique (the diverging wedge), not the metric clock-ticks of an ongoing line."
      },
      {
        "point": "The 'Event vs. Duration' distinction",
        "detail": "Demonstrate that in measures 4->5, 5 (beats 2->3), and 6 (beats 2->3), each contrary motion is a single, complete, atomic contrapuntal event bounded by rests, holds, or direction changes. Measure 1->2 is likewise a single atomic event that simply spans two beats of linear expansion. Slicing only that one event into two purely because of a passing eighth note is an analytical inconsistency."
      }
    ],
    "tone_and_suspicion_management": "Maintain total scholarly rigor, collegiate warmth, and high theoretical vocabulary. Frame the shift to Z = 4 not as abandoning rigorous theory, but as moving from naive note-to-note mechanical parsing to professional voice-leading analysis appropriate for Bernstein's score."
  }
}

